<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Annunciation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Annunciation to Mary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Annunciation is the event in which, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>, the angel Gabriel told Mary that she would conceive and bear a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, through a virgin birth. Luke places it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a> in the sixth month of Elizabeth's pregnancy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>; Matthew gives a separate, briefer announcement by an angel to Joseph. Many Christians observe the Feast of the Annunciation on 25 March, nine months before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>. Gabriel's greeting forms the first part of the Hail Mary prayer. The Annunciation was a major subject in Christian art, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annunciation.jpg" alt="Annunciation" width="330" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: uploader Koperczak 08:52, 26 March 2009 (UTC), Toros Roslin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annunciation_from_13th_century_Armenian_Gospel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The hymn par excellence is the one addressed to the Theotokos, the Mother of God, for the feast of the <b>Annunciation</b> on 25 March.</blockquote><a href="/songs/akathist">Akathist Hymn</a></li></ul></section>
